Interim tap patch, not for upstream: skip only the checks where a composed
rotation lands at exactly pi and the test's numpy reference returns the other,
equivalent, sign. mrcal's own results are identical on Linux and macOS; the
reference's sign comes from roundoff, which differs on Apple Silicon. This
replaces upstream's hardware-specific skip of the mirror case. The upstream fix
should instead compare against whichever form is closer, converting the
reference gradient to match.

--- a/test/test-poseutils-near-singularity.py
+++ b/test/test-poseutils-near-singularity.py
@@ -115,6 +115,17 @@
     return wrap_r_unconditional(r, dr_dX = dr_dX)
 
 
+def representation_flipped(r, r_ref):
+    '''Whether r and r_ref are the two forms of a rotation by exactly pi
+
+    A rotation by pi about v is the same as a rotation by pi about -v, so at
+    mag(r) = pi both r and -r are correct. Which one a computation returns is
+    down to roundoff, and the reference implementation gets it from roundoff
+    that differs between platforms. Their gradients differ too, so a check of
+    one form against the other fails even though both are right
+    '''
+    return abs(nps.mag(r) - np.pi) < 1e-8 and nps.inner(r, r_ref) < 0
+
